<Docs
  title="What is Blindkit?"
  description="Connect your AI assistant to your apps, keep API keys out of its code, and choose what it can do."
  slug=""
  headings={headings}
>
  <div class="callout">
    <strong>An early version to try.</strong>
    <p>
      You install Blindkit from its source code and run it on your computer. It is not yet on npm,
      the package registry, and has not had an independent security review.
    </p>
  </div>
  <h2 id="what-it-does">What it does</h2>
  <p>
    Blindkit sits between your AI assistant and an app you want it to use. You connect the app using
    an OpenAPI file: a description of the actions its API supports. An API is how software talks to
    an app. Your assistant can then find and use those actions.
  </p>
  <p>
    For example, your AI can read a post. If it wants to create one, Blindkit asks you to approve
    that specific request by default. Blindkit adds the API key when sending the request, so your AI
    does not need to handle it. A local activity log records the request and its result.
  </p>
  <div class="doc-flow" aria-label="Request flow">
    <span>Your AI</span>
    <b aria-hidden="true">→</b>
    <strong>Blindkit</strong>
    <b aria-hidden="true">→</b>
    <span>Your app</span>
  </div>
